,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4.500553635,"data":"Conclusion:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4.500578683,"data":"  Nose size rises steadily across the lifespan: mean nasal height grows from\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4.5005836299999995,"data":"  34.5 mm (age 4-5) to 57.4 mm (age 65-80), +22.9 mm, and nasal width grows\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4.500587523,"data":"  23.8 -\u003e 37.1 mm. Over ages 4-80 the age-nose correlation is strong and positive\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4.500592214,"data":"  (height r = 0.78, one-sided randomization p = 1.5e-04; width r = 0.92; area r = 0.85),\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4.50059673,"data":"  matching the paper's ANOVA (age p \u003c 0.001) and Lock5's quoted p \u003c 0.001. Among\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4.500600706,"data":"  adult age-group means alone (18-80 y, 5 groups) nasal height is flat (r = 0.10,\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4.500604812,"data":"  one-sided randomization p = 0.42), but nasal width keeps widening significantly\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4.50060863,"data":"  (r = 0.99, one-sided p = 8.2e-03) and area trends positive (r = 0.78,\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4.500612613,"data":"  one-sided p = 0.03) - grouped means have far less power than the study's\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4.500616148,"data":"  individual-level test (p \u003c 0.001), and most of the size gain occurs up to ~18 y.\n"}
